@Tutorial(time: 40) {
    @Intro(title: "Player scene and model") {
        In this lesson, we'll build the `Player` scene from a
        [CharacterBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcharacterbody3d.html#class-characterbody3d),
        then import the axolotl 3D model included in the starter project and
        instance it inside the scene. By the end, you'll have a character in
        the viewport, idling on the spot, ready to move.

        Character bodies are complementary to the area and rigid bodies used in the
        2D game tutorial. Like rigid bodies, they can move and collide with the
        environment, but instead of being controlled by the physics engine,
        **you** dictate their movement. You will see how we use the node's unique
        features when we code the jump and squash mechanics.

        > See also: To learn more about the different physics node types, see the
        > [Physics introduction](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/physics/physics%5Fintroduction.html#doc-physics-introduction).

        When you import a 3D model into Xogot, the file becomes a scene. You
        can then instance it inside other scenes, just like any other scene
        file. But for game characters, you'll often want to apply a few tweaks
        — scale the model, rotate it to face the right direction, or set an
        animation to autoplay — without modifying the original file. Xogot
        gives you a clean way to do this: **scene inheritance**.

        We'll create a wrapper scene that inherits the `.glb` file, apply our
        tweaks there, and then instance that wrapper inside the `Player` scene.
        This keeps the original import clean and makes the tweaks easy to
        review.

        @Image(source: fix-me.png)
    }
    @Section(title: "Creating the player scene") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We'll start by creating a `Player` scene from a
            [CharacterBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcharacterbody3d.html#class-characterbody3d).

            We're also going to create a basic rig for our character's 3D model. This
            will allow us to rotate the model later via code while it plays an
            animation.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create a new scene by opening the **Document Menu** (the down-arrow
                icon) in the upper-left corner and tapping **New Scene**.

                @Image(source: new_scene.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                In the `New Scene` window set the name to `player`, pick the `Scenes` folder location and then in the *New* tab set the type to **Empty** and press **Create**

                @Image(source: new_scene.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Tap the **Create Other Node** button and select the `CharacterBody3D` node
                type to create a `CharacterBody3D` as the root node.

                @Image(source: add_character_body3D.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Rename the
                [CharacterBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcharacterbody3d.html#class-characterbody3d)
                to `Player`.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "The CharacterBody3D node renamed to Player")
            }
            Add a
            [Node3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fnode3d.html#class-node3d)
            node as a child of `Player`.
            @Step {
                Select the `Player` node in the **Scene
                Tree** and tap the **Create New Node** button (the "+" icon) to add a
                child node. Rename it to `Pivot`.

                > Note: The `Pivot` node will hold the character model. It lets
                > us rotate the model to face its movement direction without
                > fighting the physics body. We'll instance the axolotl
                > under it later in this lesson.

                @Image(source: adding_node3D.webp)
            }
            As with all kinds of physics nodes, we need a collision shape for our
            character to collide with the environment.

            @Step {
                Select the `Player` node again and add a child node
                [CollisionShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcollisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d).
                In the **Inspector**, on the **Shape** property, add a new
                [BoxShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_boxshape3d.html#class-boxshape3d).

                The Box's wireframe appears below the character.

                @Image(source: add_capsuleshape3d.webp)
            }
            The box will be the shape the physics engine uses to collide with the
            environment, so we want to pick one that will best fit the 3D model.

            @Step {
                Make the box a bit larger by dragging the orange dot in the
                viewport or editing the shape size property in the **Inspector**. 
                We want it to have a size of 1.2 on the `x-axis`, 1.0 on the `y-axis` and 2.6 on the `z-axis`.

                @Image(source: sphere_shape.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Then, move the collision shape up so its bottom roughly aligns with
                the grid's plane. It should now have a Transform position on the `y-axis` of 0.5.

                @Image(source: moving_the_sphere_up.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Save the scene as `player.tscn`.

                @Image(source: toggling_visibility.webp, alt: "Save the scene as player.tscn")
            }
            With the player scene ready, let's import the 3D model and instance
            it under the `Pivot` node.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Creating a wrapper scene from the glb") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A `.glb` file is a self-contained glTF 2.0 archive. When Xogot
            imports it, it generates a `PackedScene` you can instance or
            inherit from. Inheriting from the `.glb` lets us override
            individual properties — like a node's transform or an
            `AnimationPlayer`'s autoplay — while keeping everything else from
            the original file.

            > See also: To learn more about glTF and the other 3D formats Xogot
            > supports, see
            > [Available 3D formats](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_3d_scenes/available_formats.html).

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In the _File Tree_, locate `art/player_axolotl.glb`.
                
                First, double click the file to open the import settings pane.
                @Image(source: fix-me.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Then open the tree for the `Animation Player` and select `Idle`. Inside the settings, in the **Inspector** set the `Loop Mode` to `Linear`.
                
                Do the same for `Run`, then press **ReImport**
                @Image(source: fix-me.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Now, tap the file `player_axolotl.glb` once to select it, and in the inspector press the **Open Scene** button.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "Long-press the glb file and choose New Inherited Scene")
            }
            @Step {
                In the pop-up window select **New Inherited** to create a new copy of the scene.
                
                @Image(source: fix-me.png)
            }
            @Step {
                A new scene will open with the glb's contents as its root. Save it
                as `player_axolotl.tscn` inside the `scenes/` folder (create
                the folder if it doesn't exist yet).

                > Note: Because this scene inherits from the `.glb`, any
                > change you make here only affects instances of this wrapper.
                > The original `.glb` file stays untouched. If you re-export
                > the model from Blender later, your tweaks are preserved.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "Save the inherited scene as player_axolotl.tscn")
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Scaling and orienting the model") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            When you look at the model in the viewport, two things are likely
            off: it's very small, and it faces the wrong way. Models from 3D
            software often come in at a different scale and facing +Z, while
            Xogot's forward direction is -Z. We'll fix both with a single
            transform on the model's `Armature` node.

            > Note: In Xogot 3D, the -Z axis is "forward." Code like
            > `Basis.looking_at(direction)` rotates a node so its -Z points
            > along the given direction. If your model faces +Z by default,
            > rotating the armature 180° around Y makes it face -Z so it
            > looks the right way when you steer it with code.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In the _Scene Tree_, expand the root node and select the
                `Armature` node (the node that holds the `Skeleton3D`).

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "Select the Armature node in the Scene Tree")
            }
            @Step {
                In the _Inspector_, expand the _Transform_ section. Set the
                _Scale_ to `4` on the X, Y, and Z axes to make the axolotl
                roughly life-sized in the scene.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "Scale the Armature to 4 on all axes")
            }
            @Step {
                Now rotate the armature 180° around the Y axis so it faces -Z.
                You can either drag the red arc in the viewport, or enter
                `180` in the _Rotation_ Y field in the _Inspector_.

                > Tip: After rotating, the axolotl should now face away from
                > the camera (towards -Z). When we steer it with code later,
                > it will face the direction it moves.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "Rotate the Armature 180 degrees on Y")
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Setting an animation to autoplay") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The glb file comes with embedded animations, like `Idle` and `Run`,
            stored on an `AnimationPlayer` node inside the model. We want the
            axolotl to play `Idle` as soon as the game starts.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In the _Scene Tree_, expand the `Armature` node and select the
                `AnimationPlayer` child.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "Select the AnimationPlayer inside the Armature")
            }
            @Step {
                Open the **Animation** drawer, press the `A` icon next to the animation you want to turn autoplay on for. We will do this for `Idle`. This tells the AnimationPlayer to start playing the
                `Idle` animation as soon as the scene loads.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "Set the Autoplay property to Idle")
            }
            @Step {
                Save the `player_axolotl.tscn` scene.

                We'll use the exact same pattern — inherit the `.glb`, scale
                the armature, rotate 180° on Y, set autoplay — for the mob
                model later in the series.
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Instantiating the model in the Player scene") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Now we'll instance the wrapper scene we just built inside
            `player.tscn`, under the `Pivot` node we created earlier.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open `player.tscn`. In the _File Tree_ long press and drag `player_axolotl.tscn` 
                onto the `Pivot` node.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "Instantiate player_axolotl.tscn under Pivot")
            }
            @Step {
                Rename the instantiated node to `Character`. This name keeps
                the player and mob scenes symmetric, which will come in handy
                later.

                @Image(source: scene_structure.webp)
            }
            Your scene tree should now have a `Player` root, a `Pivot` child,
            and a `Character` child (the axolotl model).
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Resizing the collision shape to fit the model") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The `BoxShape3D` we added earlier was sized generically. We'll now
            resize it to wrap the axolotl's body, which is longer along the Z
            axis (front-to-back) than it is wide.

            > Tip: Collision shapes don't need to match the model exactly —
            > they just need to feel right when you play the game. A box is
            > simple, reliable, and performs well.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Move the `CollisionShape3D` up so the box's bottom aligns with
                the ground grid if it doesn't already, and shift it slightly forward in -Z so it
                covers the body. You can drag the gizmo in the viewport or
                enter values directly in the _Transform_ section.

                > Tip: Toggle the `Character` node's visibility with the eye
                > icon to see the collision shape clearly while you resize it.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "Position the box so it wraps the axolotl body")
            }
            @Step {
                Save the scene.

                In the next lesson, we'll code the player's movement.
            }
        }
    }
}
